      "id": "fb-001",
      "question": "What happens when a SIM goes offline?",
      "shape": "document",
      "guard": "fix",
      "blocked_on": null,
      "asked_as": "1a1db65e-52d5-493b-a1a9-7e495005530a",
      "http_status": 200,
      "seconds": 33.79,
      "answer": "When a SIM goes offline, several checks and procedures are typically followed to diagnose and resolve the issue:\n\n1. **Review Offline List**: The offline list is sorted by \"last seen\" and \"fleet importance\" to prioritize which units need immediate attention.\n\n2. **Check SIM Health and Coverage**: This involves verifying the SIM's health, checking for any roaming mismatches, and confirming that the data bundle coverage is adequate.\n\n3. **Identify Potential Issues**: Common issues that might cause a SIM to go offline include antenna problems, power instability, SIM lock or expiry, and APN misconfiguration.\n\n4. **Corrective Maintenance**: This includes checking the last message timestamp from the device, validating power input, and possibly rebooting the device to restore connectivity.\n\n5. **Connectivity Triangle**: The \"Connectivity Triangle\" standard involves ensuring proper GPS, GSM/GPRS, and power connections. A failure in any of these areas could lead to the device going offline.\n\nThese steps help in identifying and resolving the root cause of the SIM going offline, ensuring that connectivity is restored efficiently.\n\nSource: NAVAS CMS User Manual — this is the documented procedure.",
